#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139599 :tutorial/content "We can use unit conversions to convert from one set of units to another.\n\nFor instance, the speed of sound is approximately $0.34$ kilometers per second. What is this in meters per second? We can find out using the following two steps.\n\n**Step 1: Create a conversion formula.**\n\nWe are given the units\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert this into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nTo figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{s}\\cdot\\, \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\textrm{km}$ with $\\textrm{m}$. To get rid of $\\textrm{km}$, we can put $\\textrm{km}$ in the denominator so that it cancels out:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}}}{s}\\cdot \\dfrac{?}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThen, to introduce $\\textrm{m}$, we can put $\\textrm{m}$ in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}}}{s}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{s}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{km}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\n**Step 2: Use the conversion formula.**\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The speed that we wish to convert is $0.34 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{s}}$.\n- There are $1\\,000$ meters per kilometer.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.34\\frac{km}{s} &= 0.34\\frac{km}{s} \\cdot 1000\\frac{m}{km} \\\\\n&= (0.34 \\cdot 1000)\\frac{km}{s} \\cdot \\frac{m}{km} \\\\\n&= 340\\frac{m}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the speed of sound is $340$ meters per second." :tutorial/id #uuid "d1f56c71-9cb3-5a5b-881c-bfbad588ca7f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1404 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
